00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.330
Kubernetes is an
open source system

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.390
for automating deployment,
scaling, and management

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.760
of containerized applications.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.920
It can run on many
different environments,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.770
from laptops to
high-availability, multi-node

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.400
clusters, from public clouds
to on-premise deployments,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.190
from virtual machines
to bare metal.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.600
It groups containers that
make up an application

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.550
into logical units for easy
management and discovery.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.460
Kubernetes builds upon
15 years of experience

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.160
of running production
workloads at Google, combined

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.480
with best of breed ideas and
practices from the community.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.240
The goal of this hands-on
lab is for you to turn code

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.780
that you have developed into a
replicated application running

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.350
on Kubernetes, which is
running on Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:15.290
For this lab, the code will be a
simple Hello World node.js app.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.020
Here's a diagram of the various
parts in play in this lab

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.390
to help you understand how
the pieces fit together

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:21.680
with one another.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.500
Use this as a reference as
you progress through the lab.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.950
It should all make sense by
the time you get to the end.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.200
This lab will take about
an hour to complete,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.160
and you'll create a node.js
server, a Docker file that

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.360
describes the image you want
to build, a container cluster,

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.770
then a Kubernetes pod, and
then scale up your services

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.560
in case you suddenly need more
capacity for your application.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.740
The lab only scratches the
surface of this technology.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.050
And we encourage you
to explore further

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:57.809
with your own pods, replication
controllers, and services.

